===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A wide [[bowl]] for [[washing]], sometimes affixed to a wall.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Taming of the Shrew|II|1|text=First, as you know, my house within the city<br>Is richly furnished with plate and gold,<br>'''Basins''' and [[ewer]]s to [[lave]] her dainty hands;}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|John|13|5|text=After that he poureth water into a '''bason''', and began to wash the disciples’ feet, and to wipe them with the towel wherewith he was girded.}}
#* {{RQ:Smollett Travels|volume=1|letter=V|text=What then, you will say, must a man sit with his chops and fingers up to the ears and knuckles in grease? No; let those who cannot eat without defiling themselves, step into another room, provided with '''basons''' and towels: but I think it would be better to institute schools, where youth may learn to eat their victuals, without daubing themselves, or giving offence to the eyes of one another.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1923|author=w:Willa Cather|title=w:One of Ours|section=Book One, Chapter 1|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2369/pg2369-images.html
|passage=Everybody had washed before going to bed, apparently, and the bowls were ringed with a dark sediment which the hard, alkaline water had not dissolved. Shutting the door on this disorder, he turned back to the kitchen, took Mahailey’s tin '''basin''', doused his face and head in cold water, and began to plaster down his wet hair.}}
#: {{syn|en|sink}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A shallow [[bowl]] used for a single [[serving]] of a drink or liquidy food.
#* {{RQ:Austen Emma|15|text={{...}} Mr. John Knightley, ashamed of his ill-humour, was now all kindness and attention; and so particularly solicitous for the comfort of her father, as to seem—if not quite ready to join him in a '''basin''' of gruel—perfectly sensible of its being exceedingly wholesome {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1826|author=[[w:George Adam Wood|George Wood]]|title=The Subaltern Officer: A Narrative|location=London|publisher=Longman, Rees, Orme, Brown & Green|chapter=7|page=109|url=https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/100136856
|passage=They have a good '''basin''' of coffee or cocoa for breakfast {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Oliver Twist|2|text=He rose from the table; and advancing to the master, '''basin''' and spoon in hand, said: somewhat alarmed at his own temerity: ¶ ‘Please, sir, I want some more.’}}
#* '''1893''', [[w:Sir Gilbert Parker, 1st Baronet|Gilbert Parker]], “The March of the White Guard,” in ''Tavistock Tales'', New York: Tait Sons & Co., p. 27,<sup>[https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/100261129]</sup>
#*: Gaspé Toujours is drinking a '''basin''' of tea, and Jeff Hyde is fitfully dozing by the fire.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1915|author=w:Sarah Broom Macnaughtan|title=A Woman’s Diary of the War|location=New York|publisher=Dutton|year_published=1916|chapter=7|page=99|url=https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/009787387
|passage=A steaming '''basin''' of coffee or soup revived them greatly, and even having to decide which of these refreshments they would have, and helping themselves to bread, pulled them together a little.}}
# A [[depression]], natural or artificial, containing water.
#* {{RQ:Twain Tom Sawyer|31|text=This shortly brought them to a bewitching spring, whose '''basin''' was incrusted with a frostwork of glittering crystals {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1891|author=w:Frederic Farrar|title=Darkness at Dawn|url=http://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks13/1306651h.html|chapter=6
|passage=The fountains were plashing musically into marble and alabaster '''basins'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1926|author=w:D. H. Lawrence|title=w:The Plumed Serpent|url=http://www.gutenberg.net.au/ebooks03/0300021.txt|chapter=2
|passage=There was a stone '''basin''' of clear but motionless water, and the heavy reddish-and-yellow arches went round the courtyard with warrior-like fatality, their bases in dark shadow.}}
# {{lb|en|geography}} An [[area]] of land from which water [[drain]]s into a common [[outlet]]; [[drainage basin]].
#* {{quote-journal
|1=en
|date=2012-01
|author=Douglas Larson
|title=Runaway Devils Lake
|volume=100
|issue=1
|page=46
|magazine=w:American Scientist
|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2012/1/runaway-devils-lake
|passage=Devils Lake is where I began my career as a limnologist in 1964, studying the lake’s neotenic salamanders and chironomids, or midge flies. [&hellip;] The Devils Lake '''Basin''' is an endorheic, or closed, '''basin''' covering about 9,800 square kilometers in northeastern North Dakota.
|accessdate=12 May 2013
|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20120523024858/http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2012/1/runaway-devils-lake
|archivedate=23 May 2012
}}
# {{lb|en|geography}} A shallow [[depression]] in a rock [[formation]], such as an area of down-folded rock that has accumulated a thick layer of sediments, or an area [[scoop]]ed out by water [[erosion]].

